e  of value of impact that you make  as as a laboratory right and from the  basic level of  of business continuity of operating of  operations of turnaround time and cost  per test  yeah that's that's that's that's  important and essential  but it's low impact to the overall  health system but as we move up to the  hospital and the system level  really focusing on their kpis and  putting ourselves in a position  of affecting turner affecting  readmission rates and length of stay  that's where we start to create value  and if we build a system and a model  that helps us and no matter what disease  burden it is because it doesn't have to  be infectious disease  where we're really understanding and  being able to anticipate shifts in the  population  deal with crises as they come and handle  the post surveillance so we can learn  and grow and develop and get stronger as  health systems that's where labs create  maximum impact and to do that  if you have no tools right that's a  challenge if you have tools that sit in  the emr  that gets you somewhere but that doesn't  really enable you to be the best of who  you  are make your maximum contribution and  so this is about  making sure from a lab perspective  there's a dedicated strategy  and control of the tools and the  processes that enable  this type of analysis insight  development and deployment to occur and  so  that's why i'm excited to turn it over  to dr hayden who's going to talk a  little more about  this concept of more with less from a  different angle and show us  his thoughts and how to apply this this  type of thinking  in vivo dr hayden  thank you very much jeremy um  and yeah i'm happy that everyone was  able to to take the time  to to join us today i think it shows  maybe  one of two things either one you're a  chemist like me which means it's your  microbiology colleagues who are running  around crazy trying to figure this out  or hopefully it means two you recognize  that we're going to get through  t