er  so norton started this some time back we  put it up on the website to allow  people to go in and see the  cases of diagnosed influenza  a or b per certain number of residents  in their geographic area right so why do  we do this  well because as we're all learning  understanding  where communicable diseases are highly  prevalent  is an essential step to reducing the  spread  of those diseases i certainly appreciate  it because it helps me see where i might  maybe not want to go to a to a child's  birthday party for instance  but i want to mention that when we're  looking at this data  the only reason we're able to display  this in an effort to help  our community health is because it's  built around  essential accurate diagnostics  right this is the sort of communicable  disease tracker that would be fantastic  to have when we have these  new outbreaks such as covid  unfortunately we don't have those  essential diagnostics  and without that key first step as  jeremy mentioned  you can't take any of the subsequent  steps  it's also why i think that doing  molecular flu  like we do at all of our hospitals and  all of our medical group offices  is an important and worthwhile activity  yes it is substantially more expensive  but i believe that that expense is worth  it given the value and  accuracy of that result because i think  that molecular flu  is a great example of us doing  impactful testing which is really what  we want to be doing more of  so what is impactful testing  so i took a stab at a definition of that  i would consider impactful testing a  laboratory test  that is required for and leads to  improved patient care and it's really  those two elements  required for and leads to right  so required for we do a lot of testing  where the patient's care path is going  to go  regardless of what that result came back  as  and that's not impactful testing  furthermore the laboratory test has to  actually  lead to improved patient care right so  jeremy was talking about medical errors  being the third leading cause of death  you know one thing that we've learned  one of the most common laboratory  medical errors  is a failure to retrieve error that  means that there is  some essential laboratory test out there  in the patient's record very often the  electronic  medical record which is never retrieved  never acted on  and because of the failure to retrieve  and act on that result  patient suffers harm right so impactful  testing needs to think about  how can you ensure that appropriate  patient care and next steps  are actually taken  so what is an example of impactful  testing which i think we've been  able to do here at norton  so one example would be hiv testing  so for those who might not be as  familiar  there's 1.1 million people in the united  states  who have been diagnosed with hiv  now out of that just over a million  individuals  it's estimated that one in seven  are undiagnosed they're unaware of their  hiv positive  status studies have shown  that those undiagnosed individuals  account for eighty percent or more  of the new infections right  so think about that one in seven  individuals a minority  of those living with hiv are undiagnosed  and yet that small percentage of  individuals accounts for the vast  majority of new infections  with estimates showing that every  hundred undiagnosed cases of hiv  leads to eight new infections  so if we can actually test for and  diagnose people with hiv  that is a very impactful testing because  not only can we alter the care pathway  of that individual who was previously  undiagnosed  but we can also reduce the risk of them  spreading it to  others and creating new cases  this is why the u.s preventative  services task force actually  put out a recommendation that  individuals between the ages of 15 to 65  years of age  be screened for hiv regardless of  risk factors  so one of the things we wanted to do at  norton was to say  how good a job are we actually doing  screening individuals for hiv  and i want to recognize here the  tremendous work by the sdi task force  led by michelle rose who put together  this data  and have been influential in the efforts  i'm about to talk about  so when michelle and her team looked at  the patients that we were actually  testing  what they found was that about  three-quarters of the individuals tested  were women even though men accounted  for three-quarters of our positive  results  so once again a quarter of the patients  were actually testing are men  but out of that small percentage who are  tested they account for the majority  of new positive cases and this was a  strong indication to us  that while we were doing a better job  screening women especially  in collaboration with our ob gyn  colleagues  we were missing a lot of individuals who  should be tested  and so we set about to implement  universal screening  so how do we set up our hiv universal  screening  well the first thing that we did is we  called it an opt-out  program right this is not that  individuals  choose to be screened it's that  individuals  have a choice to not be screened and  that language is  really important when your frontline  providers are  discussing with patients whether or not  they want to be screened  right telling them that everyone is  being screened unless they object  people are far less likely to object  so to implement this we actually build a  best practice  advisory recommendation in the epic  emr and you can see that hiv screening  comes up  as part of your general assessment and  triage  of an individual and if the patient does  not  opt out then the lab tests will be done  automatically so what impact did this  have  in the norton system  well we implemented the universal  screening  round about september and you can see  right away  that our volume of testing increased  substantially right  we started to use a lot more hiv test  reagents  and the lab budgets were certainly  impacted  and i think