e to consider  from a lab leadership perspective to be  able to make massive impact  and i'm going to start with a slide  about my good friend michael ela for  those of you on the last call you know  this is the ceo of nokia  and his famous statement about we didn't  do anything wrong but somehow he lost  again the central theme here that we  have to carry through  is this is not about doing something  wrong it's about ensuring that we're  doing the next  right thing that we're preparing for  what's next that we're developing new  skills  new competencies new new processes that  facilitate  forward movement in healthcare it's not  enough just to do a good job  we always have to be considering how  we're getting better how we're  developing and how we're preparing for  what's next  and that the last time we talked a  little bit about how health care  was changing and why and we re round  time back to the year 2013  where in fact actually in the us medical  error  was the third leading cause of death and  our essential theme here was not that  our u.s health care system's bad  because it's not but what it was a  central theme is that we can do  better there are things that happen  that are diagnostic errors that are just  general error in  our error in general that are impacting  patients in a negative way  and so what this was is a call to action  for us to rethink what we were doing as  a health system  redefine what quality is and for the  for everyone with all the constituents  within the health system to take a step  forward to change  to develop and to grow and so that  actually spurred my first point that we  have to carry forward from last  last time which is the metrics that we  should be looking at the things that we  should use to assess  how good we're doing are not turnaround  time  and they're not cost per test they're  centered around the cms metrics that  were developed  into after 2013 because of this  medical error impact to the leading  cause of dea