nt to see it  integrated into their daily lives  now this is going to be actually  accelerated from since the last time we  we talked because you can imagine with  this covid crisis  this is going to facilitate a new need  for people to want to experience  care without having to go to a facility  to experience it  this this crisis is actually going to  accelerate a change in telemedicine  this crisis is going to accelerate a  change in alternative care models  a change in triaging of patients before  they get to the hospital host of other  things that are gonna  that are gonna happen simply accelerated  by this crisis but it's gonna fit  into this domain of the fact that people  want it integrated into their daily  lives  the final topic that we hit on was kind  of this construct of okay well how does  that work  and you know and trying to think  differently than just hey we have our  health  our health system because we actually  have two other components that we have  to figure out how do we integrate  and align with when you think about the  social determinants you have all these  community resources that are there to  support the well-being of the population  of the community  and then you have this concept of the  home and what really is the daily life  of people and the question really is is  how do we connect all of these things  how do we make them work in concert and  effectively how do you create health  care without walls  again not having the foresight of the of  the cova crisis  but this is exactly what is stressing  our system right now  is how could we manage patients  who are self-quarantined in a way where  kind of healthcare becomes wi-fi around  them  and if you think about if we already  were at this point where we could  coordinate all these resources  we would be much more effective at  dealing with this crisis  because again what we'd be able to do is  is  deal with patients as they self  quarantine at home and mobilize all the  community resources to support them  so the sense of isolation the sense of  fear could go away because the sense of  support  would be there unfortunately this  doesn't exist today  and and why that doesn't exist is simply  because  as you see on the slide here is these  there needs to be connective points  bridges that connect this external data  into the health system  and then bridging from the health system  to the home  into the daily life of individuals if  these bridges existed we would be in a  much better position  to react and so the whole central theme  of that was just this  that from a lab perspective it's not  sufficient  for us to measure the future around  automation  automation's going to be important it's  going to be necessary  but it is not who we are the future of  lab medicine  is going to be measured by our ability  each of us  to step in and take a leadership role in  the decision making that happens  in the clinical pathway strategically  operationally not just within the four  walls of the system  but in a system without walls and so  what we postulated in the last round  was you know there are five things that  you know that  steps that laboratories should be taking  to facilitate  developing competency and capability in  this which we're not going to review  today  but as we go back into the covet crisis  i want to bring back my good friend  mr elop okay now again as we kind of  approach this  we have to be thinking about um is we're  going to look back at some point at this  crisis  and it's going to teach us some very  valuable lessons about what we could  have done differently to be ready  what we could have done differently to  be empowered and  this is also an excellent opportunity  for us to educate the broader health  community  about the value of the lab because as we  all know  lab medicine should be the leader in  this crisis  and there's a number of of of scientific  publications that walk through  pandemic and epidemic after pandemic  after epidemic  and walk through the the role the  laboratory should play the role  diagnostics should play  and as this highlights in in this  article  that you know clearly that the  diagnostics is fundamental to the  approach  and it actually highlights the fact that  there are developmental needs that need  to take place  from a diagnostic network perspective  for us to be  effective at dealing with pandemics and  epidemics  and that's not development of new  technologies that's not just the  development of informatics  it includes people and process in  addition to technology  it's important and as  the organization find kind of laid out  it's not just that we're  when when we're at the heat of of  of the crisis the diagnostic plays a  role there's a surveillance front end  and back end that's really really  important so at some point  this could very easily die away as we  get new tests out for cobit  that the people go okay well now we can  do testing diagnostics fades to the back  that's that can't be the case in fact  on the on the post surveillance side we  have a big role to play  and we need to be prepared to play it  and being prepared for  a future element or a future emergence  of a new infectious disease  of how we would manage that and so  staying on top  of changes that we need to make in our  operating model are going to be really  essential  in the end it all boils down to the fact  that an accurate diagnosis  is the first step to getting effective  treatment the world health organization  knows that  healthcare knows that and we're at the  center point  of it and so part of our our job has to  be how do we use the available resources  and tools and data that's what dr  hayes is going to talk about to help us  get to that accurate diagnosis  faster and more efficiently at the end  of the day  the things that you i would encourage  you to think about as we go through the  rest of this presentation is the  following  is there's there is there is a scale  of value of impact that you make  as as a laboratory right and from the  basic level of  of business continuity of operating of  operations of turnaround time and cost  per test  yeah that's that's that's that's  important and essential  but it's low impact to the overall  health system but as we move up to the  hospital and the system level  really focusing on their kpis and  putting ourselves in a position  of affecting turner affecting  readmission rates and length of stay  that's where we start to create value  and if we build a system and a model  that helps us and no matter what disease  burden it is because it doesn't have to  be infectious disease  where we're really understanding and  being able to anticipate shifts in the  population  deal with crises as they come and handle  the post surveillance so we can learn  and grow and develop and get stronger as  health systems that's where labs create  maximum impact and to do that  if you have no tools right that's a  challenge if you have tools that sit in  the emr  that gets you somewhere but that doesn't  really enable you to be the best of who  you  are make your maximum contribution and  so this is about  making sure from a lab perspective  there's a dedicated strategy  and control of the tools and the  processes that enable  this type of analysis insight  development and deployment to occur and  so  that's why i'm excited to turn it over  to dr hayden who's going to talk a  little more about  this concept of more with less from a  different angle and show us  his thoughts and how to apply this this  type of thinking  in vivo dr hayden  thank you very much jeremy um  and yeah i'm happy that everyone was  able to to take the time  to to join us today i think it shows  maybe  one of two things either one you're a  chemist like me which means it's your  microbiology colleagues who are running  around crazy trying to figure this out  or hopefully it means two you recognize  that we're going to get through  this challenging time and we can't lose  sight of doing those important  long-term things which are going to make  us so much better  6 12 18 30 months from now  and i think keeping that eye on the  long-term goals and  strategically moving forward is really  really important  so thank you all for coming and as here  we said we're going to be talking a  little bit more about  doing more impactful testing with less  waste  just for disclosure support for this  program is provided by abbott  and i am presenting at the request of  abbott  so some of you might not be familiar  with norton healthcare  so norton healthcare is situated in the  louisville kentucky area  although our primary service area is  about 16 counties  with about one and a half million people  with almost three quarters of our  patients coming from the jefferson  county  area and we have a range of health  conditions which are  impacting the health of our communities  and these health conditions then become  our areas of priority  these are areas where we want to focus  additional efforts  not surprisingly heart disease cancer  diabetes are at the top  we also have norton children's hospital  and so children's health  is always an essential area of priority  for our health system and in case you  aren't too  familiar or you're still not terribly  interested  in what norton healthcare does i will  mention  that our system of five hospitals seven  cancer center infusion labs and  dozens of norton medical group and icc  areas  also cover the louisville area  which accounts for the vast majority of  the world's bourbon  so in case you're like me and you often  find yourself needing to go home and get  a drink  at the end of the days recently remember  we're  helping to keep keep that bourbon  distilling  one of the things that i've really liked  about norton  is that as a system we've tried to focus  on doing more for the health of our  communities  i think one good example of this would  be our flu tracker  so norton started this some time back we  put it up on the website to allow  people to go in and see the  cases of diagnosed influenza  a or b per certain number of residents  in their geographic area right so why do  we do this  well because as we're all learning  understanding  where communicable diseases are highly  prevalent  is an essential step to reducing the  spread  of those diseases i certainly appreciate  it because it helps me see where i might  maybe not want to go to a to a child's  birthday party for instance  but i want to mention that when we're  looking at this data  the only reason we're able to display  this in an effort to help  our community health is because it's  built around  essential accurate diagnostics  right this is the sort of communicable  disease tracker that would be fantastic  to have when we have these  new outbreaks such as covid  unfortunately we don't have those  essential diagnostics  and without that key first step as  jeremy mentioned  you can't take any of the subsequent  steps  it's also why i think that doing  molecular flu  like we do at all of our hospitals and  all of our medical group offices  is an important and worthwhile activity  yes it is substantially more expensive  but i believe that that expense is worth  it given the value and  accuracy of that result because i think  that molecular flu  is a great example of us doing  impactful testing which is really what  we want to be doing more of  so what is impactful testing  so i took a stab at a definition of that  i would consider impactful testing a  laboratory test  that is required for and leads to  improved patient care and it's really  those two elements  required for and leads to right  so required for we do a lot of testing  where the patient's care path is going  to go  regardless of what that result came back  as  and that's not impactful testing  furthermore the laboratory test has to  actually  lead to improved patient care right so  jeremy was talking about medical errors  being the third leading cause of death  you know one thing that we've learned  one of the most common laboratory  medical errors  is a failure to retrieve error that  means that there is  some essential laboratory test out there  in the patient's record very often the  electronic  medical record which is never retrieved  never acted on  and because of the failure to retrieve  and act on that result  patient suffers harm right so impactful  testing needs to think about  how can you ensure that appropriate  patient care and next steps  are actually taken  so what is an example of impactful  testing which i think we've been  able to do here at norton  so one example would be hiv testing  so for those who might not be as  familiar  there's 1.1 million people in the united  states  who have been diagnosed with hiv  now out of that just over a million  individuals  it's estimated that one in seven  are undiagnosed they're unaware of their  hiv positive  status studies have shown  that those undiagnosed individuals  account for eighty percent or more  of the new infections right  so think about that one in seven  individuals a minority  of those living with hiv are undiagnosed  and yet that small percentage of  individuals accounts for the vast  majority of new infections  with estimates showing that every  hundred undiagnosed cases of hiv  leads to eight new infections  so if we can actually test for and  diagnose people with hiv  that is a very impactful testing because  not only can we alter the care pathway  of that individual who was previously  undiagnosed  but we can also reduce the risk of them  spreading it to  others and creating new cases  this is why the u.s preventative  services task force actually  put out a recommendation that  individuals between the ages of 15 to 65  years of age  be screened for hiv regardless of  risk factors  so one of the things we wanted to do at  norton was to say  how good a job are we actually doing  screening individuals for hiv  and i want to recognize here the  tremendous work by the sdi task force  led by michelle rose who put together  this data  and have been influential in the efforts  i'm about to talk about  so when michelle and her team looked at  the patients that we were actually  testing  what they found was that about  three-quarters of the individuals tested  were women even though men accounted  for three-quarters of our positive  results  so once again a quarter of the patients  were actually testing are men  but out of that small percentage who are  tested they account for the majority  of new positive cases and this was a  strong indication to us  that while we were doing a better job  screening women especially  in collaboration with our ob gyn  colleagues  we were missing a lot of individuals who  should be tested  and so we set about to implement  universal screening  so how do we set up our hiv universal  screening  well the first thing that we did is we  called it an opt-out  program right this is not that  individuals  choose to be screened it's that  individuals  have a choice to not be screened and  that language is  really important when your frontline  providers are  discussing with patients whether or not  they want to be screened  right telling them that everyone is  being screened unless they object  people are far less likely to object  so to implement this we actually build a  best practice  advisory recommendation in the epic  emr and you can see that hiv screening  comes up  as part of your general assessment and  triage  of an individual and if the patient does  not  opt out then the lab tests will be done  automatically so what impact did this  have  in the norton system  well we implemented the universal  screening  round about september and you can see  right away  that our volume of testing increased  substantially right  we started to use a lot more hiv test  reagents  and the lab budgets were certainly  impacted  and i think