ump in on this  dr hayden and feel free to  feel free to pile on here give me my  perspective  you know i've been um i've been okay  i've been preaching i guess  this message for i would say going on  almost uh 12 years right um you know  long  you know looking at the healthcare  ecosystem understanding the central  theme of the lab and all the impact  that it can make and it's a really good  question uh what i would say  is you know is is we use the metaphor of  preaching to the choir um it's you know  it's not enough that the message we're  here is is we're delivering is being  delivered across the board  but it's kind of the choir's  responsibility to get people to church  right there's a message in here that  maybe is is  is latent it's not the messages the lab  is valuable  that's not the message that's not the  central theme of the gospel  the central theme of the gospel is a lab  needs to do some different things  to facilitate you know making this  change because we've been saying this  for a very long period of time all of us  collectively  understanding the value that it creates  and talking about it  but at the end of the day talking about  it's not sufficient  and continuing to do what we've always  done and and  and stay under the operating model and i  know this is easier said than done so  please don't take this  as of course this is easy uh staying  under the operating model and the  in the in the in the metrics that we've  been playing from  um yeah is is what's hurting us so  with the call to actions here are a  couple  one is take some time to actually build  a written  strategy for the lab  in a way that's presentable to the  c-suite  so that what you've got is you've got a  way to demonstrate  not not defend the value you're creating  today  but to illuminate the value incremental  value  you could create what the c-suite and  executives and payers  they don't want to hear about how good  they have it they want to hear somebody  step in with solutions that help them  move  forward they create incremental value  and so  you know kind of message one is you know  build  build a strategy a road map an  identification  of where this new value can be created  of where how the lab could be used  differently  and so that's kind of one central thing  the second central theme  is rethink the operating model that you  have  in in terms of you know not only  building a strategy but  but how do you execute day in and day  out uh  what are the what are the things that  you do what are the what are the  activities that you have  uh that enable you to step outside the  four walls of the lab into the patient  pathway  into working directly with clinicians  hand in hand  what are the what are those what are  those behaviors that are different and  new  that increase your visibility and the  third domain  is centered around the tooling and  control as we  just mentioned earlier is is how do you  locate  that those things in your hands i  you know i often joke with people like  i'm horrible at diy  and you know in some kind in some cases  the emr is like  my dad who is a who's a master craftsman  trying to explain  over skype how i'm supposed to put  together a cabinet  now of course he can tell me i can give  me some insight and i can ask him some  questions and you know and he can point  me to the instruction manual  but i don't necessarily have the  tactical skill for it  and so what i would encourage you to  think about is the third call to action  is  try to ensure that you've got the  tooling inside the four walls of the lab  and the lab's control  to facilitate whether that's clinical  decision support whether it's business  intelligence however you want to you  take a look at it  so that you're able to use your data  you're able to access it you're able to  control  for how you look at it uh and you're  able to be in a position  to take an active role with the  executive on the strategy or with the  clinicians downstream  so it's not as much as it is kind of  preaching to the choir and needing the  choir to get people to church  the gospel is slightly different the  gospel is not where valuable we know  that  the gospel is hey let's change let's  build strategy  written strategy aligned to the  executives so that they can see  the value we could create that's  incremental  but let's outline and start to execute  different behaviors that make us visible  that  help us integrate into the patient  pathway side by side with our clinicians  and with our patients  and that third domain is let's tool up  the lab differently  let's make sure that we have control and  we have the tools required  to actually create this value uh and i  know that's easier said than done and i  don't i don't mean to be  you know to make it just oversimplify it  those are real challenges that i know  many of you have tried  but at the end of the day telling people  the lab's valuable  is not going to stimulate any  development or investment in the lab  us acting our way into a new realm of  being  is the only pathway that's going to end  up with people  really genuinely recognizing what we do  i don't know  dr hayden if you have anything more to  add to that or any additional  perspective  i i think it's well said from you  i think the one thing i would add is  that  i always have to remind myself and i  think we as laboratories need to  remind ourselves that it's not enough  to be speaking this message to the  c-suite  we need to speak in a language that they  understand  and what matters to them you know  it's very easy to sort of dismiss  some of the concerns they might bring up  or objections or  um you know to think that you're just  miscommunicating but  really effectively communicating to them  is understanding  sort of you know as jeremy was saying  what are their strategic priorities  what do they value when they look at  something how are they thinking  about it and making sure that you're  doing your best to talk their language  and align what you are doing  in the laboratory with what they want  done  and so you know i think i think it's a  challenging conversation  i think it's happening i think that  there are things both sides can do  to make it probably more impactful but  certainly from the lab  and making sure that you are speaking  their language  and communicating in a way that they  understand and you're aligned with your  institutional priorities um is something  that we can do to better communicate  that  thank you dr hayden and thank you jeremy  both for your  insight on our q a today do either of  you have any final comments for  our audience  just uh thanks for taking the time  especially at a time like this  yeah i would just echo that and thank  you everybody for what you're doing out  there to  make our community safe and get through  this and you know as  we kind of hit on a little bit at the  beginning is you know let's  let's uh let's all keep helping each  other out and uh  we're gonna get we're gonna get to the  other end of this stronger as a  discipline and stronger as a country so  thank you so much thank you both for  today  and before we go i'd also like to thank  our audience for joining us today and  for their interesting question  we had quite a few questions we were  unable to answer today  and those questions that we were not  able to answer and any of that come in  during the on-demand period  they will be addressed by our speakers  via the email address you provided  at the time of registration one final  thank you to jeremy shubert and dr  joshua hayden again for their time today  we'd also like to thank libraries and  our sponsor abbott diagnostics for  underwriting today's educational webcast  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  mavericks will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  you will now be redirected to the  registration page  for chapter three clinical care  integration  broadcasting live next monday march 30th  at 8 a.m pacific time 11 a.m eastern  time  we hope to see you there have a great  day
 

